# Shell Working Directory

When running a terminal command that needs to execute in a specific directory (e.g. the
Godot project root for `res://`-relative commands like running GUT tests or the screenshot
tool), set the shell tool's `working_directory` parameter to that path.

Do **not** prefix the command with `cd /path && ...`.

```bash
# ❌ BAD
cd /Users/andrewhay/github/ivy && /Applications/Godot.app/Contents/MacOS/Godot --headless ...

# ✅ GOOD — working_directory: /Users/andrewhay/github/ivy
/Applications/Godot.app/Contents/MacOS/Godot --headless ...
```

Exception: if a task genuinely requires chaining multiple commands across different
directories in one call (e.g. `cd a && ./build.sh && cd ../b && ./deploy.sh`), `cd` is fine
there — this rule only targets the common case of running a single command that just needs
a different starting directory than the current one.
